@database "openai_api"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: openai_api.guide 1.0 (30.09.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "OpenAI-compatible API"
@{h1}OpenAI-compatible API

This plugin puts the agents behind the OpenAI @{b}Responses@{ub} and
@{b}Chat Completions@{ub} API. Any OpenAI client -- the @{tt}openai@{utt}
SDK, Open WebUI, LibreChat, n8n, an IDE plugin -- can then talk to an agent
as if it were a model: the model name is the agent's name, and the agent's
own prompt, tools, sub-agents and memory do the work. A Responses
conversation is an ordinary session of the calling user and shows up in the
web UI; Chat Completions calls are stateless and leave nothing behind. The
plugin has no tools, no hooks and no panel.

@{code}
For users
  @{"  Connecting a client  " link client}
  @{"  The endpoints  " link endpoints}
  @{"  How a request becomes a turn  " link turn}
  @{"  Structured output  " link structured}
  @{"  Stored conversations  " link conversations}
  @{"  Errors  " link errors}

For agents and developers
  @{"  What the model sees  " link model}
  @{"  Setting it up  " link setup}
@{body}
@endnode

@node client "Connecting a client"
@{h1}Connecting a client

@{table}
Setting | Value
Base URL | http(s)://<host>/plugins/openai_api/v1 (the instance name is the path segment)
API key | the user's ScarabHive API key, or an access token
Model | the name of an agent, as GET /models lists it
@{body}

A user gets an API key with @{tt}POST /auth/api-key@{utt} while logged in,
or with @{tt}agent-cli users generate-api-key <user>@{utt}. Clients send it
as @{tt}Authorization: Bearer <key>@{utt}; @{tt}X-API-Key@{utt} works too.
The plugin declares @{tt}accept_api_keys@{utt} in its security config: of
all plugin routes, only its routes take an API key; every other plugin route
stays tokens-only. Every call runs as the key's user -- their sessions,
their role. With auth switched off every caller is @{tt}anonymous@{utt}.

@{code python}
from openai import OpenAI

client = OpenAI(base_url="http://localhost:8000/plugins/openai_api/v1", api_key="<API key>")

first = client.responses.create(model="chat_agent", input="Remember the code word QUARTZ-17.")
second = client.responses.create(model="chat_agent", previous_response_id=first.id,
                                 input="Which code word did I give you?")
print(second.output_text)

for chunk in client.chat.completions.create(model="chat_agent", stream=True,
                                            messages=[{"role": "user", "content": "Hello!"}]):
    print(chunk.choices[0].delta.content or "", end="")
@{body}
@endnode

@node endpoints "The endpoints"
@{h1}The endpoints

All under @{tt}/plugins/<instance>/v1@{utt}.

@{table}
Endpoint | What it does
GET /models | the agents offered, sorted by name (@{tt}owned_by: scarabhive@{utt}, @{tt}created: 0@{utt})
GET /models/{model} | one of them, or 404 model_not_found
POST /responses | one agent turn on a stored conversation (or a throwaway one with store: false)
POST /chat/completions | one agent turn, stateless: the history comes with the call, no session is kept
@{body}

@{h2}Which agents are offered

The agents the web UI lists (agents whose @{tt}metadata.visibility@{utt} is
@{tt}ui@{utt} or @{tt}both@{utt}), narrowed by the plugin's
@{tt}agents@{utt} and @{tt}blocked_agents@{utt} patterns and by each
agent's role gate (@{tt}metadata.min_role@{utt}): an agent the caller's role
does not reach is not listed, and asking for it by name is the same 404
@{tt}model_not_found@{utt} as for a model that does not exist.

@{h2}POST /responses

@{table}
Field | Taken as
model | the agent; required for a new conversation, optional when continuing (then it must be the conversation's)
input | a string (one user message) or a list of message items; required
instructions | a string, put in front of the turn (see @{" How a request becomes a turn " link turn})
previous_response_id | continue the conversation of that response -- its latest response only
store | default true; false runs on a throwaway session and records no id
stream | true: Server-Sent Events (below)
text.format | structured output (see @{" Structured output " link structured})
background | true is refused (400)
tools | a non-empty list is refused (400): the agent brings its own tools
@{body}

The answer is a @{tt}response@{utt} object with one output message: the
agent's final message, as it wrote it (Markdown, not the web UI's HTML).
@{tt}usage@{utt} sums every LLM call of the turn; cached and reasoning
tokens are always reported as 0.

Streamed, the events come in this order: @{tt}response.created@{utt},
@{tt}response.in_progress@{utt}, @{tt}response.output_item.added@{utt},
@{tt}response.content_part.added@{utt}, any number of
@{tt}response.output_text.delta@{utt}, then
@{tt}response.output_text.done@{utt}, @{tt}response.content_part.done@{utt},
@{tt}response.output_item.done@{utt} and @{tt}response.completed@{utt} --
or @{tt}response.failed@{utt} for a run that failed, or an @{tt}error@{utt}
event for a turn refused after the stream began.

@{h2}POST /chat/completions

@{table}
Field | Taken as
model | the agent; required
messages | the conversation, last message from the user; required
n | only 1
stream | true: @{tt}chat.completion.chunk@{utt}s, then @{tt}data: [DONE]@{utt}
stream_options.include_usage | adds a last chunk with usage and no choices
response_format | structured output
tools | a non-empty list is refused (400)
@{body}

The answer is one choice with @{tt}finish_reason: stop@{utt}. A failed
stream sends an @{tt}{"error": ...}@{utt} chunk and then
@{tt}[DONE]@{utt}.

@{h2}Ignored

Sampling parameters (@{tt}temperature@{utt}, @{tt}top_p@{utt},
@{tt}max_tokens@{utt}, @{tt}text.verbosity@{utt}, ...) and every other field
not named above are ignored: the agent's LLM profile decides.

@{h2}The request body

JSON only: any other Content-Type is a 415 -- a form or text post is what a
page of another origin could send with a logged-in user's cookie. A body
over 16 MiB is a 413 before it is held in memory whole.
@endnode

@node turn "How a request becomes a turn"
@{h1}How a request becomes a turn

@{bullet}The last user message is the turn. The user and assistant messages
before it become the earlier turns of the conversation (Chat Completions:
all of them; Responses: those in @{tt}input@{utt}, on top of the stored
conversation).
@{bullet}@{tt}system@{utt} and @{tt}developer@{utt} messages, and the
Responses @{tt}instructions@{utt}, reach the agent in front of the turn,
under the line @{tt}Instructions from the client application:@{utt}. The
agent keeps its own system prompt.
@{bullet}Unlike at OpenAI, instructions stay in a stored conversation: they
are part of its turn. The same instructions sent again on a later turn (the
Agents SDK sends them every time) are not added again; changed ones are.
@{bullet}Only text. Content may be a string or a list of @{tt}text@{utt},
@{tt}input_text@{utt} or @{tt}output_text@{utt} parts. Image parts, tool
messages, function-call items and other roles are refused with a 400 naming
the field -- never silently dropped.
@{bullet}A new stored conversation is named, as the web UI names one, by
the first 50 characters of the user's first message -- not by the
instructions in front of it.

@{h2}What a stream carries

A stream's deltas are the work as it happens: what every LLM call of the
turn writes, the calls set apart by a blank line -- the steps of a
multi-step run (a note before a tool call, say), and a call asked again
after it broke off. The deltas of a multi-step turn are therefore more than
its final message. A Responses stream ends on the final message
(@{tt}response.output_text.done@{utt} and the events after it); a Chat
Completions stream has no final text, its deltas are all a client gets.

What of a call's content never came as a delta follows it: the rest a
client salvaged at the end of a stream, or -- when the turn's last call ran
on a model that does not stream -- the whole final message as one delta at
the end.

A structured turn streams differently (see
@{" Structured output " link structured}).

@{h2}A client that leaves

A client that disconnects stops the agent: a stream notices at once, a JSON
request asks the connection every second (and once more before its turn is
kept). The run's cancellation token is cancelled first, so an agent inside
a tool call stops too; the plugin then waits up to 12 seconds for the run to
end. The conversation stays busy until the run has really stopped.
@endnode

@node structured "Structured output"
@{h1}Structured output

@{code python}
class Trip(BaseModel):
    city: str
    days: int

trip = client.chat.completions.parse(model="chat_agent", response_format=Trip,
                                     messages=[{"role": "user", "content": "Plan a weekend trip."}])
print(trip.choices[0].message.parsed)
print(client.responses.parse(model="chat_agent", input="Plan one.", text_format=Trip).output_parsed)
@{body}

@{bullet}Chat Completions @{tt}response_format@{utt} and Responses
@{tt}text.format@{utt} take @{tt}json_schema@{utt} (@{tt}name@{utt} and
@{tt}schema@{utt}; @{tt}strict@{utt} and @{tt}description@{utt} optional)
and @{tt}json_object@{utt}; @{tt}text@{utt} changes nothing.
@{bullet}The format holds for the agent's @{b}final@{ub} answer. The steps
before it call the agent's tools as always.
@{bullet}Where the agent's model takes structured output (its model entry
declares @{tt}capabilities.structured_output@{utt}), the format goes to the
provider as a field of its own; otherwise it is described to the model in
the conversation. Either way the answer is checked against the schema, sent
back once with what is wrong with it, and a turn whose answer still does
not match fails with @{tt}500@{utt}, code
@{tt}structured_output_invalid@{utt} and the header
@{tt}x-should-retry: false@{utt} -- the run already had its correction
round, and the SDK's automatic retries would buy two more whole runs for the
same verdict.
@{bullet}The answer is the JSON as the agent wrote it, a Markdown fence
around the whole answer taken off, so the SDK's @{tt}parse()@{utt} helpers
work.
@{bullet}Streamed, a structured turn sends its answer as @{b}one@{ub} delta
once it has been checked, and nothing of the steps before it: the joined
deltas are the JSON. Meanwhile the stream sends an SSE comment
(@{tt}: keep-alive@{utt}) every 15 seconds, so a proxy does not drop a long
run.
@{bullet}An agent that cannot hold its answer to a format at all -- one
whose run takes no format, a state machine behind the stategraph facade --
is refused before it runs: 400, @{tt}unsupported_parameter@{utt}.

@{h2}The schema subset

A strict subset of JSON Schema, as OpenAI's strict mode has one:
@{tt}type@{utt}, @{tt}properties@{utt}, @{tt}required@{utt},
@{tt}additionalProperties@{utt}, @{tt}items@{utt}, @{tt}prefixItems@{utt},
@{tt}enum@{utt}, @{tt}const@{utt}, @{tt}anyOf@{utt}, @{tt}$defs@{utt},
@{tt}$ref@{utt}, @{tt}description@{utt}, @{tt}title@{utt},
@{tt}pattern@{utt}, @{tt}format@{utt} (not checked), the four number
bounds, @{tt}multipleOf@{utt}, @{tt}minLength@{utt}/@{tt}maxLength@{utt},
@{tt}minItems@{utt}/@{tt}maxItems@{utt}. @{tt}default@{utt},
@{tt}examples@{utt}, @{tt}$comment@{utt}, @{tt}deprecated@{utt},
@{tt}readOnly@{utt} and @{tt}writeOnly@{utt} are taken out. A root
@{tt}$schema@{utt} may name 2020-12, 2019-09 or draft-07; a draft-07
@{tt}definitions@{utt} is taken as @{tt}$defs@{utt}. The schemas pydantic
and the openai SDK write for plain models are inside it; @{tt}oneOf@{utt},
@{tt}uniqueItems@{utt} and @{tt}minProperties@{utt} are not.

@{table}
Limit | Value
schema size | 100 000 characters
nesting | 10 levels
properties | 5000
enum / const values | 1000
one pattern | 1000 characters
fan-out through anyOf and $ref | 1000 subschemas
answer size | 1 000 000 characters
@{body}

Anything outside is a 400 @{tt}invalid_value@{utt} naming the keyword and
where it stands; a missing @{tt}name@{utt} or @{tt}schema@{utt} is
@{tt}missing_required_parameter@{utt}, a value of the wrong kind
@{tt}invalid_type@{utt}. Nothing outside the schema is ever fetched.

@{h2}The checker

The schema is a client's input, so it is built and every answer is checked
in a separate worker process: at most two at once, one per user at a time,
each request under a 5-second deadline past which the worker is killed and
the request fails closed. A request waits at most 30 seconds for a worker;
a checker that is busy or broke answers @{tt}503@{utt},
@{tt}structured_output_unavailable@{utt} -- retryable.
@endnode

@node conversations "Stored conversations"
@{h1}Stored conversations

A Responses call with @{tt}store@{utt} left on runs on a stored session of
the calling user. It is listed in the web UI and can be continued there;
what the web chat adds becomes part of the conversation, and the next API
turn from the latest response id builds on it.

@{bullet}@{b}Only the latest response continues.@{ub} A session is one line
of turns; continuing from an older response would silently build on turns
the client did not mean, so it is a 409.
@{bullet}@{b}Another user's response id@{ub} is unknown: 404
@{tt}previous_response_not_found@{utt}.
@{bullet}@{b}A conversation no longer stored@{ub} -- deleted in the web UI,
archived, unreadable -- is not continued: 404
@{tt}previous_response_not_found@{utt}, before the agent runs.
@{bullet}@{b}One turn at a time.@{ub} A second request on the same
conversation while one runs is a 409. The conversation is also held against
runs elsewhere (session presence, as the web chat holds its sessions): one
that the web chat or an agent-cli run has in hand is refused with a 409.
@{bullet}@{b}A turn is kept only once it is delivered.@{ub} The session is
saved, then the response id recorded. Any other end -- the run failed or was
stopped, the client left before the answer went out, the save failed --
puts the conversation back as it was before the turn (messages and session
variables), so a retry from the last response does not build on a turn the
client never got. A new conversation is deleted then; a continued one is
only restored. Messages the user appended meanwhile stay, and the sessions
of agents the run called as tools are put back with it where only this turn
wrote them.
@{bullet}@{b}A client that leaves while its answer is being sent@{ub} is not
seen by the server: the turn is kept, the client never got the id, and a
call from the response before it is refused (409, not the latest). Such a
client starts a new conversation.

@{h2}Throwaway sessions

Chat Completions and @{tt}store: false@{utt} run on a throwaway session
that is dropped when the turn ends. Sub-agents such a turn left running in
the background are stopped -- nobody can continue the session to read their
answers. The record a sub-agent manager writes for the parent
(@{tt}Coordinator Session@{utt}) is deleted; the sub-agents' own sessions
stay on disk, hidden below it, as when a session is deleted in the web UI.

@{h2}Shutdown

When the plugin stops it waits up to 12 seconds for runs still stopping,
so their conversations are put back before the process ends. After that it
records nothing: a stored Responses call is a 503.
@endnode

@node errors "Errors"
@{h1}Errors

Errors are OpenAI-shaped, @{tt}{"error": {"message", "type", "param",
"code"}}@{utt}, so an SDK raises the matching exception. A streamed request
is refused with the same status as a JSON one, before its stream starts.

@{table}
Status | Type / code | When
400 | invalid_request_error | a malformed or unsupported field; @{tt}param@{utt} names it
401 | invalid_api_key | no valid user (see below)
403 | permission_error | the conversation belongs to another user
404 | model_not_found | an unknown agent, or one the caller may not run
404 | previous_response_not_found | an unknown or foreign response id, or its conversation is gone
409 | conflict | the conversation is answering another request, or another run has it
409 | invalid_request_error | previous_response_id is not the conversation's latest
413 | invalid_request_error | the body is over 16 MiB
415 | invalid_request_error | the Content-Type is not application/json
500 | server_error | the agent run failed; the message is the run's
500 | server_error / structured_output_invalid | the answer did not match the format after its correction (x-should-retry: false)
503 | server_error | the plugin is stopped, the app is still starting, or the schema checker is unavailable
@{body}

An unexpected failure inside the plugin answers
@{tt}internal error: <exception type>@{utt} -- its text is only logged,
also in a stream. A missing or wrong key is usually refused by the app's
auth layer before the request reaches the plugin: a 401 with
@{tt}{"detail": "Authentication required for this plugin endpoint"}@{utt},
which the openai SDK raises as its @{tt}AuthenticationError@{utt} all the
same.

In a stream, a run the agent refused before it started (its role gate, a
conversation held for another user) is a Responses @{tt}error@{utt} event,
or an error chunk in Chat Completions, with the codes above; a run that
failed after it started is @{tt}response.failed@{utt} (code
@{tt}server_error@{utt}) or an error chunk carrying the structured-output
code where there is one.
@endnode

@node model "What the model sees"
@{h1}What the model sees

@{h2}The turn

The agent runs exactly as in the web UI, with its own system prompt, tools
and LLM profile. What the client adds reaches the model as ordinary
conversation:

@{bullet}the client's earlier turns (Chat Completions @{tt}messages@{utt},
Responses @{tt}input@{utt} items) as user and assistant messages before the
turn;
@{bullet}the client's system/developer messages and instructions inside the
user message, in front of the user's text:

@{code text}
Instructions from the client application:
Answer in one sentence.

What is the capital of France?
@{body}

The agent's own system prompt is never replaced, so a client cannot turn
an agent into a different one by instructions alone -- only steer it.

@{h2}Structured output

With a format, the model either gets it as a provider field on every call
of the turn (the same field on every call, so the cached prefix holds), or
-- where its model takes no such field -- a description of the format as a
note of the run. A wrong answer is sent back once with what is wrong with
it.

@{h2}Tokens and cache

The plugin adds no text of its own beyond the instructions block. In a
stored conversation that block becomes part of the history (sent once per
change of instructions), so later turns stay a prefix of the ones before and
the prompt cache holds. A structured turn continued without its format
changes the request once, because the provider field is part of the prefix.
Chat Completions sends the whole history with every call, so each call is a
fresh run on it.

@{h2}Known gaps

@{bullet}No images, audio or files; no client-side function calling.
@{bullet}No background mode, no @{tt}GET /responses/{id}@{utt}, no
conversation items API.
@{bullet}Continuing from an older response (a branch) is refused, not
forked.
@{bullet}Runs through this API are not web UI jobs: the chat panel does not
follow them live, and a stored conversation appears after its turn.
@{bullet}With session presence off nothing holds a conversation against a
run in another process.
@{bullet}A Gemini model's thought summaries reach a stream's deltas (its
clients stream them as text); the final texts and every JSON answer do not
have them.
@{bullet}Structured output never answers a @{tt}refusal@{utt}: a model's
refusal is no answer in the format, and the turn fails.
@endnode

@node setup "Setting it up"
@{h1}Setting it up

The entry shipped in @{tt}config/plugins.yaml@{utt}:

@{code yaml}
plugins:
  servers:
    openai_api:
      type: openai_api
      enabled: true
@{body}

No allowlist line: the plugin has no tools. The API is up once the app has
started with the entry enabled.

@{h2}Settings

Flat in the server entry, next to @{tt}type@{utt} -- not in a
@{tt}config:@{utt} block.

@{table}
Key | Default | Meaning
agents | (all) | fnmatch patterns of the agents offered as models; empty: every agent the web UI lists
blocked_agents | (none) | fnmatch patterns never offered, even if @{tt}agents@{utt} matches
responses_db | data/openai_api/responses.db | the SQLite file mapping response ids to sessions; a path starting with data/ lands in the data directory
@{body}

@{code yaml}
    openai_api:
      type: openai_api
      enabled: true
      agents: ["chat_agent", "research_*"]
      blocked_agents: ["sysadmin_*"]
@{body}

@{h2}Where data lives

The conversations are the users' ordinary sessions. The plugin's own file,
@{tt}responses.db@{utt}, holds per response its user, session and agent,
per conversation its latest response, and the instructions it was last
given. It is opened on first use and closed when the plugin stops.

@{h2}Dependencies

@{tt}anyio@{utt} (it comes with Starlette). The @{tt}openai@{utt} SDK is
only needed by clients and the tests.
@endnode
